    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "```\nour probability will rain on Saturday is 0.6\nour probability will rain on Sunday is 0.25\nprobability neither will rain on Saturday is 0.3\nprobability neither will rain on Sunday is 0.75\nprobability it will rain on both days is 0.18 = 18 percent\n``` To solve this problem, we will use the concept of independent probabilities in probability theory.\n\n1. **Identify the given probabilities:**\n   - Probability it will rain on Saturday, \\( P(S) = 0.6 \\)\n   - Probability it will rain on Sunday, \\( P(U) = 0.25 \\)\n   - Since the events are independent, the probability it will not rain on Saturday is:\n     \\[\n     P(\\text{not } S) = 1 - P(S) = 1 - 0.6 = 0.3\n     \\]\n   - Similarly, the probability it will not rain on Sunday is:\n     \\[\n     P(\\text{not } U) = 1 - P(U) = 1 - 0.25 = 0.75\n     \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the probability that it does not rain on both days:**\n   - The events \"it does not rain on Saturday\" and \"it does not rain on Sunday\" are also independent. Therefore, the probability that it will not rain on both days is the product of the individual probabilities of each event not raining:\n     \\[\n     P(\\text{not } S \\text{ and not } U) = P(\\text{not } S) \\times P(\\text{not } U) = 0.3 \\times 0.75 = 0.225\n     \\]\n\n3. **Find the probability that it will rain on at least one day:**\n   - The probability of rain on at least one day is the complement of the probability that it does not rain on both days:\n     \\[\n     P(\\text{rain on at least one day}) = 1 - P(\\text{not } S \\text{ and not } U) = 1 - 0.225 = 0.775\n     \\]\n\n4. **Convert the probability to a percentage:**\n   - Finally, we convert the probability to a percentage:\n     \\[\n     \\boxed{77.5}\n     \\]",
